{
  "name": "ru-rules-v1",
  "description": "Russian morphology learned by the SAME conditioned-rule inductor as English — only the vowel primitive was extended to Cyrillic. Nominative plural: masculine takes -ы, but -и after the velar/hushing consonants к г х ж ш ч щ (the classic ы/и spelling rule, here discovered as a closed consonant class on the stem's last letter, so it generalises to any masculine noun); neuter -о -> -а, -е -> -я. Hard stems only; irregular/fleeting-vowel/indeclinable nouns (стул->стулья, носок->носки, метро) are out of scope.",
  "lattice_id": "schema-math",
